{-
   `@heap` on a producer's own storage - `doc/inflight/Design-Iterator-Objects.md` §6.1's second fix,
   and the accepting side of `Reject.Borrow.yana`.

   # resolver: ownership

   A record moves with whatever holds it, because every move here is bytewise - so a borrow into its
   own bytes, or an address taken of one of them, points at where the value used to be. `@heap` is
   the way out the refusal names: the member then lives in a run of its own that does not move, and
   the record holds its **pointer**.

   Three things follow, and all three are what this file runs:

    - `f$new` allocates one run per placed member, for the object's whole life, so the record owes a
      free whatever its members are - which is also what stops `copy(it)` on one.
    - the step reaches the value through the pointer, exactly as it reaches a capture held by
      address;
    - a **borrow** of a placed member is a payload member too, held as its address and written where
      the producer's own binding is established. A value cached at the step's entry would be the
      contents from before that write, and one cached at the write would not reach the resume blocks
      the dispatch enters directly - so it is read at every use.

   The record is printed in the `.ssa.expect` beside this file, where `placed` and `borrowed` are the
   two words that say which of the three a pointer member is.

   § A placed member is a member because of where it lives

   And not because of what crosses a hand-over, which is the fourth thing and the one this file used
   to assume rather than assert. The payload is otherwise "every local live across some `yield`", and
   `@heap` is orthogonal to that: a producer may place a value, hand out its address, and then read
   the local only before its first `yield`. `placedEarly` is that shape, and `placedOnce` reads the
   allocator's bump pointer around fifty rounds of it.

   Left out of the payload, the local was an ordinary frame slot of the **step** - and a step's frame
   is entered once per element, so the allocation landed in the dispatch block and happened per step
   call, with no `free` in the module at all. Nothing above caught it because every producer here
   authors a `Drop`, and a teardown forces a local into the record by the other route.
-}

import Native

data Cell {value: Int}

instance Drop(Cell):
    fn drop(->value: Cell): {}

-- §6.1's first shape, honoured: a borrow of the producer's own storage, held across a `yield`.
iter fn selfBorrowed(n: Int) -> Int:
    let @heap base = Cell {value: n}
    let held = base

    yield held.value
    yield base.value + held.value

-- And its second: a raw address, which no later rule catches once it is an integer.
iter fn addressed(n: Int) -> Int:
    let @heap base = Cell {value: n}
    let at = addressOf(base)

    yield n
    yield base.value + 1

data Buffer {slots: [U8 *8]}

fn fill(&b: Buffer):
    let &i = 0 :: Int

    while i < 8:
        b.slots[i] = truncate(i + 65)
        i = i + 1

{-
   §6.1's `bytes()`, walked by index.

   The buffer is the producer's own storage and lives in a run, so an object of this may be moved
   between two steps and the next step still finds it. `bytesByItems` below is the same example
   written the way the design writes it - with a nested `for b in items(buffer)` - which arrived with
   §3.2's first step.
-}
iter fn bytes(n: Int) -> Int:
    let @heap &buffer = Buffer {slots: ([0] :: [U8 *8])}
    fill(buffer)

    let &i = 0 :: Int

    while i < n:
        yield buffer.slots[i] :: Int
        i = i + 1

fn borrowing() -> Int:
    let &it = selfBorrowed(3)
    let &packed = 0 :: Int

    for v in it:
        packed = packed * 10 + v

    return packed

fn addressing() -> Int:
    let &it = addressed(4)
    let &packed = 0 :: Int

    for v in it:
        packed = packed * 10 + v

    return packed

{-
   Stepped **after the object was relocated**, which is the property the placement buys.

   `let &->moved = it` consumes the object into a second name, so the record's bytes travel; what the
   placed member holds is a pointer into a run that did not travel, and the borrow the producer
   established before the move is still a borrow of the same value. One step is taken first, so what
   moves is a record suspended past index zero with its run already allocated.

   Within one frame, which is where the placement question is: a signature may name `bytes.Iter`
   since stage 2 (`iter/Name.yana`), and what a run-holding member needs asserted is that the record's
   bytes may move while the run does not.
-}
fn placedAndMoved() -> Int:
    let &it = bytes(4)
    let &packed = 0 :: Int

    if it.next() is Just(first):
        packed = first - 65

    let &->moved = it

    for b in moved:
        packed = packed * 100 + b - 65

    return packed

fn fillSlots(&b: [U8 *8]):
    let &i = 0 :: Int

    while i < 8:
        b[i] = truncate(i + 65)
        i = i + 1

{-
   §6.1's `bytes()` as the design writes it - `for b in items(buffer)` inside the producer, which is
   a nested traversal and therefore a payload member holding `items(...).Iter`.

   **A fixed array is not a self-borrow**, and that is worth stating because the design's example
   reads as though it must be. `bufferBytes(xs: [U8 *8])` below takes its container by a shared loan, and a loan
   of a trivially copyable whole value is physically a **copy** (`IterCaptureKind::Value`) - so what
   the child's record holds is the eight bytes, not a pointer into the outer one, and the refusal has
   nothing to fire on. `@heap` is not needed here and would buy nothing.

   `bufferBytes` and `each` rather than `items`, since J129: `items` and `Chunked.chunks` now publish
   their container's origin (`self: source'c`, `'c`), which makes the container a `'T` reference -
   so over a fixed array they *are* a self-borrow - and an object nesting a traversal that hands over
   a slot-named scalar does not convert yet (J129 §7). `each` below is `items` as it was written
   before, unqualified.

   `heapedItems` below is the shape that really is one.
-}
iter fn (Chunked(c, a)) each(self: c) -> a:
    for chunk in chunks(self):
        for i in 0 .. length(chunk):
            yield chunk[i]

iter fn bufferBytes(xs: [U8 *8]) -> U8:
    for i in 0 .. 8:
        yield xs[i]

iter fn bytesByItems() -> Int:
    let &buffer = [0] :: [U8 *8]
    fillSlots(buffer)

    for b in bufferBytes(buffer):
        yield b :: Int

{-
   And the self-borrow §6.1 draws, with the fix it names.

   An `Array(Int)` is not trivially copyable, so `items(mine)` borrows it and the child's record
   holds the lender's address - which, for a local of the producer, is an address *into the outer
   record*. Without `@heap` that is refused by name (`Reject.Nested.yana`'s `overOwn`); with it the
   container lives in a run of its own that the record only points at, so the child's borrow survives
   the object moving and the position gets an origin set of the outer record's own.
-}
iter fn heapedItems() -> Int:
    let @heap mine = [1, 2, 3]

    for v in each(mine):
        yield v

fn walkingBytes() -> Int:
    let &it = bytesByItems()
    let &packed = 0 :: Int

    for b in it:
        packed = packed * 10 + b - 65

    return packed

fn walkingHeaped() -> Int:
    let &it = heapedItems()
    let &packed = 0 :: Int

    for v in it:
        packed = packed * 10 + v

    return packed

{-
   A placed local the producer reads only **before** its first hand-over.

   `Plain` authors no `Drop` on purpose: a teardown is the other thing that makes a local a payload
   member, so a type that owes one would mask the question this producer asks. `peek` is `@noinline`
   for the same kind of reason - a read the optimizer can fold away is a local that was never live -
   and it takes the value by a shared loan so that the placement is what the address comes from.
-}
data Plain {value: Int}

@noinline
fn peek(p: Plain) -> Int = p.value

iter fn placedEarly(n: Int) -> Int:
    let @heap base = Plain {value: n}
    let seen = peek(base)

    yield seen
    yield n + 1

fn stepping(n: Int) -> Int:
    let &it = placedEarly(n)
    let &packed = 0 :: Int

    for v in it:
        packed = packed * 10 + v

    return packed

{-
   One run per object and one free per object, read off the allocator rather than counted.

   The mark is taken after a first round, because the first allocation of a program is what asks for
   a page and every one after it comes out of the free list - `iter/Erased.yana`'s placement check
   states the same thing at more length. What fifty rounds then assert is that the bump pointer did
   not move: a run allocated per step call and never freed moves it by fifty times three.
-}
fn placedOnce() -> Int:
    let first = stepping(3)

    let cold = bitcast(heapNext) :: Size

    let &i = 0 :: Int
    while i < 50:
        if stepping(3) != 34 then return 1
        i = i + 1

    if (bitcast(heapNext) :: Size) != cold then return 2

    return first

fn main() -> Int = placedOnce() + borrowing() + addressing() + placedAndMoved() +
                   walkingBytes() + walkingHeaped()
